		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " An assignment target's `.prop` lookups never break — prettier's `printMemberExpression`",
			"start": 10,
			"end": 100
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " inlines every lookup when the assignment's left is not a plain identifier",
			"start": 102,
			"end": 178
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " (member.js `shouldInline`), so the whole target prints as one unbreakable unit.",
			"start": 180,
			"end": 262
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " What the rule measures is the target's own head, `<target> <operator>`.",
			"start": 264,
			"end": 338
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " head exactly 100 — the value drops to its own line",
			"start": 341,
			"end": 394
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " head 101 — the target has no break point, so it stays intact and overflows",
			"start": 504,
			"end": 581
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " Contrast: a computed lookup IS a break point, so an over-width head sheds width",
